WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:02.410
LAK LAKSHMANAN: How many
of the tables in your data

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:05.980
warehouse have location
information in them?

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:10.360
Perhaps you record the latitude
and longitude of your vehicles

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.820
or packages over time.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.000
Or maybe you have the
addresses of your customers

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.740
and suppliers.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.500
Maybe you recorded
customer transactions

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.140
and you could join it
against another table that

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.940
has store locations.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.210
Not only is location
information very common,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:32.229
many business decisions
revolve around location--

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.350
who to send out coupons
to, whether the package

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:36.910
will reach on time.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.910
Therefore, geospatial
analysis is

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.550
quite common in data analytics.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:48.430
9% of .sql files in GitHub
carry out spatial queries.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:52.450
That's why I'm happy to
show you a new feature that

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:58.030
makes analyzing and visualizing
geospatial data in BigQuery

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.740
very easy and highly performant.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.270
GHCN Daily is a public
data set of weather data,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:13.240
and the stations table contains
latitude and longitude columns.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.210
In another table,
I have information

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:21.130
about postal codes used by the
United States Postal Service.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.810
There are two
columns in this table

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.610
that are of interest here.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.270
The WKT, or Well-Known
Text, is a format

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.520
for representing geographies.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.220
In this case, it is a polygon.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.400
The postal code,
called the ZIP code,

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.260
is the second column
that we're interested in.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.850
So let's run a query that
finds all the weather

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.170
stations within 10
kilometers of a ZIP code.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.800
I'm pulling the ZIP code
polygon and the weather station

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.030
location.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:58.720
And then in my WHERE clause, I
am filtering using ST_DWithin--

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.760
distance within.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.700
I'm checking whether
the station's

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.180
latitude and longitude, which
form a geographic point--

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:12.400
whether that point is within
10 kilometers of the ZIP code

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.530
polygon.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.840
And if you look at
the results, there

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.960
are two stations that
are inside the polygon

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.810
itself, so their distance is 0.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.580
And there are 28 total stations
within 10 kilometers of the ZIP

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.910
code.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.590
This is great but if
you have spatial data,

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:33.940
you want to visualize it.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.220
You want to visualize
it on a map.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.670
The BigQuery Geo Viz is a
convenient way to do this.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:45.250
So here I have the same
query in the Geo Viz.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.250
I can select the
geometry column and then

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.510
click Run to get a map
of the weather stations.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.470
I can also style the points
based on some other column

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.320
in the data.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.670
Here's another query that
shows off the ability

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.190
to style things in the map.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.780
This time, I'm joining the
census data set of population

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.210
with ZIP code boundaries.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.420
Let's make the polygon
column-- the WKT column--

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:18.940
the geography and
change the colors

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.240
and opacity of the polygons
based on population.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.040
As you can see, BigQuery GIS
provides a convenient, powerful

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.310
way to incorporate
spatial information

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.640
into your decision-making,
even as your data sets

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.140
growing into the petabytes.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

